1
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:17,590
Hi, sweetheart.
How'd it go with you today?

2
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:20,876
Swell, and you?

3
00:02:21,040 --> 00:02:25,636
Great. I closed the store early
and I went for a jog.

4
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:31,312
Glen?

5
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:34,677
- Is something wrong?
- No, nothing's wrong.

6
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:36,669
But you're drinking,

7
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:38,796
and you haven't had a drink
in nine months.

8
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:39,949
I've had a couple.

9
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:41,951
Now, why don't you have one
with me?

10
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:43,959
No.

11
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:46,192
I'll get it. No, I'll get it.

12
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,433
Surprise.

13
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:53,352
How you doing, Glen? Hi, Laurie.

14
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:55,590
Hi, Michael. Why didn't you tell us
you were coming?

15
00:02:55,760 --> 00:02:58,149
Didn't know myself.
Last-minute sales meeting.

16
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:00,675
What's been going on here?
You look like hell.

17
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:02,398
Well, thanks a lot.

18
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:04,357
Have you been hitting
the booze again, Glen?

19
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:07,034
- I thought you were all over that.
- Once in a while.

20
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,430
Once in a while? Hey.

21
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:11,477
Let me take this bum out
for a few hours.

22
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:15,713
A little talk, a little dinner.
I'll straighten him out.

23
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:17,598
Okay.

24
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,832
I'll have him back in mint condition.

25
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:22,956
Let's go, you bum.
I'll buy you dinner.

26
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:48,633
- Laurie?
- I'm in here.

27
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,995
Could you tell me, please,
when your big sister gets home?

28
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:02,470
Because I want to apologise to her.

29
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,116
And tell her how much I love her.

30
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:32,551
Don't answer it.

31
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,875
Could be important.

32
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,438
Hello?

33
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,673
Yes?

34
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:43,914
Oh, no.

35
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:46,633
Yes.

36
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:51,029
Okay, I'll be right there.
Yeah. Thank you.

37
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:52,838
What's wrong?

38
00:04:56,080 --> 00:04:59,675
The burglar alarm at the shop.
That was the security guard.

39
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:01,796
They want me to go down there
and meet the police.

40
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:07,869
Sweetheart, why don't you
go back to sleep, okay?

41
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:11,956
I will be back as soon as I can.

42
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,869
You did hear me, didn't you?

43
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:19,836
When I said that I loved you?

44
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:45,956
- Hold it, ma'am.
- That's my house.

45
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:48,793
- Where's my husband?
- I'm sorry. Please, just step back.

46
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:50,518
Glen?

47
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:54,908
Mrs. Palmer,
can I speak to you a minute?

48
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:57,548
I'm afraid I have some bad news.

49
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:03,438
No. Is it Glen? Tell me it's not Glen.

50
00:07:03,600 --> 00:07:06,876
They took him to the morgue
a few minutes ago.

51
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:09,513
No.

52
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:11,952
No.

53
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:14,718
No.

54
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,356
Are you Mrs. Palmer?

55
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:20,629
Would you come with me, please?

56
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,631
Hey, lieutenant.

57
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:25,548
- Who are you?
- Dan Abrams for <i>The Examiner.</i>

58
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:27,597
Well, later.

59
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:29,318
Watch your head, please.

60
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:55,478
Now, Mr. Harper, about this robbery,
you were the only eyewitness.

61
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:58,029
- Is that correct?
- Yes, sir.

62
00:07:58,200 --> 00:07:59,553
Now, you testified

63
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:03,235
that you saw a soldier running
from the Humbolt cement plant

64
00:08:03,400 --> 00:08:05,868
at 1 1 :30 at night. Is that correct?

65
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:10,670
- It certainly is.
- Now, just how dark was that alley?

66
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:13,354
Not so dark that I couldn't see
the soldier running away

67
00:08:13,520 --> 00:08:15,476
carrying the payroll sack.

68
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:18,791
He wasn't holding it up so high
you couldn't see his face?

69
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:20,598
No, sir.

70
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:26,398
- How's your eyesight?
- Twenty-twenty.

71
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,232
And you were how far away?

72
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,355
About 30 yards.

73
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:35,240
And that's the gentleman
you saw right there?

74
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:37,991
Yes, that's him.

75
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:40,596
Would you stand up, sir?

76
00:08:41,720 --> 00:08:42,835
And you, please, sergeant.

77
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:46,513
And you, sir.

78
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:49,799
And you, sir.

79
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:54,271
You sure it wasn't
one of these gentlemen?

80
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:04,752
Yes, I am.

81
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:07,476
Mr. Harper,
making a positive identification

82
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:11,474
is one of the most
difficult things any of us can do.

83
00:09:11,640 --> 00:09:14,871
The gentleman at the defence table
who you identified as the thief

84
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:18,191
is not the defendant,
Sergeant James Norris.

85
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,229
That gentleman is Sergeant Norris.

86
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:27,069
No further questions, Your Honour.

87
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:31,717
Mr. Matlock,
can I speak to you for a minute?

88
00:09:31,880 --> 00:09:34,030
- Sorry, I don't have a minute.
- What about right now?

89
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:35,519
I'm on my way to the men's room.

90
00:09:35,680 --> 00:09:38,433
- This is very important.
- So is this.

91
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:48,198
Mr. Matlock, everything they have
on her is circumstantial.

92
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:50,316
You could drive a truck
through their whole case.

93
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:53,756
Well, that may be true, <i>Mr.--</i>
What did you say your name is?

94
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:55,558
Abrams. Dan Abrams.

95
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,870
Well, I appreciate your good opinion,
Mr. Abrams,

96
00:09:58,040 --> 00:09:59,473
but I'm gonna have to decline.

97
00:09:59,640 --> 00:10:02,313
- I'm leaving town in the morning.
- What for?

98
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:05,590
Matter of fact, I'm going on vacation.

99
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:08,718
Vacation? But Laurie Palmer's
about to be set up

100
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:10,950
for a crime she didn't commit.

101
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:13,759
There are other lawyers in Atlanta
besides me. Good ones too.

102
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:15,799
Not as good as you.

103
00:10:15,960 --> 00:10:19,839
I don't know how strenuously I wanna
argue with you on that, Mr. Abrams,

104
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:21,069
but the fact remains--

105
00:10:21,240 --> 00:10:24,630
Mr. Matlock, I'm a writer.
I'm writing a piece on you.

106
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,078
A profile for the
<i>Sunday Supplement.</i>

107
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:30,949
For the last six weeks,
I've done research on Ben Matlock.

108
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:33,588
I believed you were a great fighter
for justice.

109
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:36,479
I know you take cases
sometimes for pay,

110
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:40,553
or because something interests you,
or when clearly, there's an injustice.

111
00:10:40,720 --> 00:10:43,871
And, sir, clearly,
there's been an injustice here.

112
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:46,793
Fifteen minutes, that's all I'm asking.

113
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:49,599
Allow me to write this profile
on a great man

114
00:10:49,760 --> 00:10:52,752
who, in truth, uses the law for right.

115
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:54,512
Please.

116
00:10:55,320 --> 00:10:56,309
Where is she?

117
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:00,589
She had the opportunity.
The house was locked, dead bolt.

118
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:03,593
Only somebody with a key
could have started the fire,

119
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:05,671
gone out and locked the door.

120
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:08,195
The only people with keys
were her and her husband.

121
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:10,032
Next, the m.o.

122
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:13,397
Well, gas escaping from both the oven
and the hot-water heater.

123
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:16,028
You try and tell the jury
that's a coincidence.

124
00:11:16,200 --> 00:11:17,269
Want a motive?

125
00:11:17,720 --> 00:11:21,599
Half a million dollars life-insurance
policy on the husband in her name.

126
00:11:21,760 --> 00:11:23,079
- Like it so far?
- Not much.

127
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:24,673
Well, how about this?

128
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:28,071
There's no record of any alarm
going off in her shop that night,

129
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:29,719
so there goes her alibi.

130
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:32,758
Ben, we got her, open and shut.

131
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:35,310
Sure looks like it.

132
00:11:35,880 --> 00:11:39,156
- Can I see her?
- Help yourself.

133
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:41,675
This won't take long.

134
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:54,032
So I waited and I waited,
and nobody came.

135
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:57,317
So I went home.

136
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:00,354
Do I have to talk about this?

137
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:04,518
No one saw you
while you were waiting?

138
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:07,114
No, nobody saw me.

139
00:12:07,280 --> 00:12:11,239
I mean, I don't think so. I don't know.

140
00:12:12,280 --> 00:12:13,713
Try to remember.

141
00:12:16,360 --> 00:12:20,433
Mr. Matlock, Glen is dead.

142
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:26,633
You're gonna have to
prepare a defence.

143
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:39,596
I loved him so much.

144
00:12:44,680 --> 00:12:48,036
Nobody can do anything
about that now.

145
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:52,954
How can this happen?

146
00:12:54,800 --> 00:12:57,519
Let's see what we can do
about you.

147
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:07,159
Bail is denied.
Defendant is remanded to custody.

148
00:13:07,320 --> 00:13:12,394
The preliminary hearing
is set for the 1 6th in this department.

149
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:14,633
I'm sorry.

150
00:13:24,040 --> 00:13:26,873
Excuse me. Sorry.

151
00:13:28,640 --> 00:13:31,393
Yo, Ben. Ben.

152
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:34,950
Mr. Matlock,
could I have your autograph?

153
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:36,314
What are you talking about?

154
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:41,590
Matlock has grown so successful

155
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:45,469
that he now limits his practise
to defendants of murder cases.

156
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:48,837
He refers to his clients as, quote,
''An exclusive club,

157
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,037
but one you wouldn't wanna join
if you can help it,'' unquote.

158
00:13:52,200 --> 00:13:53,952
Let me see that.

159
00:13:54,280 --> 00:13:56,635
How about that? They spelled
your name right and everything.

160
00:14:23,880 --> 00:14:26,872
- You set me up.
- Everything I said was the truth.

161
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:28,917
''Fighter for justice
wherever there's injustice,

162
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:31,389
uses the law for the right.''
You set me up.

163
00:14:31,560 --> 00:14:34,393
Why didn't you tell me you're just
another reporter looking for a story?

164
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:36,630
I didn't tell you I like
strawberry ice cream, either,

165
00:14:36,800 --> 00:14:38,233
but what I said was the truth.

166
00:14:38,400 --> 00:14:40,789
I do want you on this case,
and that story's legitimate news.

167
00:14:40,960 --> 00:14:43,872
Damn it, you used me
just to get a headline for this story.

168
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:46,873
I'm on her side as much as you. You
are gonna represent her, aren't you?

169
00:14:47,040 --> 00:14:49,349
I ought to represent
suing your hind-end off.

170
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:51,795
I'm a reporter. I was on that story,
I was at the fire,

171
00:14:51,960 --> 00:14:54,190
I was there when she came home.
I'm the one that told her

172
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,874
her husband was dead.
I was there when they arrested her.

173
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:02,358
- You have notes?
- Sure, I have notes.

174
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:04,996
- Let me see them.
- What for?

175
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:07,833
I'm gonna need all the information
I can get.

176
00:15:08,480 --> 00:15:09,879
- I want the story.
- No.

177
00:15:10,040 --> 00:15:12,679
Don't worry. I won't publish anything
that'll jeopardise your case.

178
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:13,875
No, not a chance.

179
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:21,550
You promise?
Not one word without my okay?

180
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:25,508
Long as I get the story first,
I don't mind waiting a while to publish.

181
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:29,434
All my notes, right here.

182
00:15:30,560 --> 00:15:32,278
What do you say?

183
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:44,599
That's right, honey.
Probably at least another week.

184
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:48,116
The police are still talking to me,
and then the trial.

185
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:50,079
Yes.

186
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:52,716
Yes, I will.

187
00:15:52,880 --> 00:15:54,916
Love you too. Bye.

188
00:15:55,840 --> 00:15:57,717
Sorry, my wife works nights
as a nurse.

189
00:15:57,880 --> 00:15:59,518
If I don't talk to her now,
she's asleep,

190
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:01,830
and then later,
I'm out with customers.

191
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:03,718
Well, maybe you'll get together
later on.

192
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:07,793
Oh, I hope so. I'm tired of looking
at myself in the mirror.

193
00:16:09,640 --> 00:16:12,200
Tell me about
your brother's marriage.

194
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:14,717
Well, I'll be real honest with you,
Mr. Matlock.

195
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:17,110
I'm not crazy about my sister-in-law.

196
00:16:18,040 --> 00:16:19,871
But Glen married her,

197
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:23,279
and she's in enough trouble
without my shovelling dirt on her.

198
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:24,873
Yeah.

199
00:16:25,480 --> 00:16:30,349
Well, tell me what you know.
Things have a way of popping up.

200
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:34,669
Well, I'll tell you something
I didn't tell the police.

201
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:36,910
My brother was going
to divorce Laurie.

202
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:39,275
She said they were crazy
over one another.

203
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:40,634
He was going to leave her.

204
00:16:41,640 --> 00:16:44,108
- Glen said that?
- The night he--

205
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:49,994
The night he died,
we went out to dinner together.

206
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:54,469
He said he wasn't happy with her.
He couldn't take it anymore.

207
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:56,358
He was going to leave.
It was finished.

208
00:16:57,600 --> 00:17:00,592
And you think she killed him then
because of that?

209
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:05,957
Couple hours later, he's dead.

210
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:15,033
Well.

211
00:17:16,640 --> 00:17:19,029
Thank you for your cooperation.

212
00:17:25,480 --> 00:17:27,675
Where'd you first meet
your husband?

213
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:31,071
Glen moved to Atlanta
about a year ago,

214
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:35,233
and a friend of mine worked
at his office. She introduced us.

215
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:37,753
You ever meet his family?

216
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:42,838
No. Just his brother Michael.

217
00:17:43,520 --> 00:17:45,715
I'm having a hard time
getting a personal history

218
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:47,950
- on your husband.
- What do you mean?

219
00:17:48,120 --> 00:17:51,078
Well, there's no personnel file on him
in his company.

220
00:17:51,240 --> 00:17:52,309
Personnel director told me

221
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:54,232
the company president
hired him directly.

222
00:17:54,680 --> 00:17:56,671
Yeah, I think that's right.

223
00:17:57,120 --> 00:17:58,519
What else do you know about him?

224
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,432
We didn't have any secrets.

225
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:05,672
Other than his brother, have you ever
met any of his family or old friends?

226
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:08,029
No, why?

227
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:12,552
Well, somebody killed your husband,
so somebody had a motive.

228
00:18:12,720 --> 00:18:15,280
Then it would sure help
to look into his past.

229
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:18,637
I don't know.
Did you talk to his brother Michael?

230
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:24,149
Mr. Matlock?

231
00:18:25,880 --> 00:18:28,348
- Yeah?
- Where are you headed?

232
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:31,318
I thought I'd take a look
at the crime site.

233
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:34,870
- Come on, I'll give you a lift.
- Okay.

234
00:18:35,040 --> 00:18:36,632
Here it is.

235
00:18:40,080 --> 00:18:43,072
- You're kidding.
- This is my car.

236
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:47,673
Does it run?

237
00:18:47,840 --> 00:18:50,149
I work for a major newspaper.
When I have to be somewhere,

238
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:54,154
I have to be somewhere.
Of course it runs.

239
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:58,633
- I don't believe this.
- Believe it, it's a good car.

240
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:00,711
Okay.

241
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:02,950
You gotta reach in
through the window.

242
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:27,948
I think I may be on to something.

243
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:29,473
About your car?

244
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:31,478
No.

245
00:19:31,920 --> 00:19:35,276
Laurie doesn't seem to know
too much about her late husband.

246
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:37,476
Neither does anybody else.

247
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:41,472
There's no personnel file
at his company,

248
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:44,234
which is peculiar enough.

249
00:19:45,200 --> 00:19:47,430
But something's
just not right about him.

250
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:53,517
The diploma on his office wall says
he graduated from Michigan State.

251
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:55,477
You wanna keep your eyes
on the road?

252
00:19:55,640 --> 00:19:57,392
Okay.

253
00:19:57,560 --> 00:20:02,680
The registrar's office says yes,
he was a student.

254
00:20:02,840 --> 00:20:06,037
But the Journalism Department
says otherwise.

255
00:20:06,200 --> 00:20:07,679
Road.

256
00:20:07,840 --> 00:20:10,400
Journalism says otherwise, huh?

257
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:15,630
There's a certificate in his office
that says he won a top award,

258
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:19,951
but the story on the award that year
says it was won by somebody else.

259
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:25,032
Well, maybe it's just poor journalism.
There's a lot of that going around.

260
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:30,274
He wasn't in the yearbook, either.

261
00:20:31,240 --> 00:20:32,559
Road.

262
00:20:33,080 --> 00:20:35,036
You know what I think?

263
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:40,109
Something not kosher
about our corpus delicti.

264
00:21:08,320 --> 00:21:11,118
Excuse me, how did this happen?

265
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:12,759
You don't know about this?

266
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:15,992
No, I just this second got home.
I work for an airline.

267
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:19,277
I've been in Paris and Rome,
and I had a two-day layover in London.

268
00:21:19,440 --> 00:21:20,919
I'm Sally Duncan.

269
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:23,150
I'm Dan Abrams.
I'm a reporter for <i>The Examiner.</i>

270
00:21:23,320 --> 00:21:24,753
This is Ben Matlock.
He's an attorney.

271
00:21:24,920 --> 00:21:26,672
- Hello.
- Hi.

272
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:30,437
- When did you leave?
- Last Monday night.

273
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:33,512
- The fire was that night.
- What time did you leave?

274
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:37,116
About 1 1 :1 5.
My flight was a 1 a.m. departure.

275
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:39,551
Did you see anything unusual,
anything at all?

276
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:42,630
Well, I don't know.

277
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:46,395
As I was getting ready to leave,
I saw some people go into the house.

278
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:48,915
Three men, and, well,
they sounded pretty drunk.

279
00:21:49,080 --> 00:21:51,878
Two of them, anyway.
They were carrying a third man.

280
00:21:52,040 --> 00:21:54,190
It looked like he had passed out.

281
00:21:55,160 --> 00:21:56,957
Did you recognise any of them?

282
00:21:57,600 --> 00:21:59,989
No, it was too dark.

283
00:22:00,160 --> 00:22:01,229
But you know what?

284
00:22:01,400 --> 00:22:04,710
Just as I was leaving in my cab, I saw
them come down from the house.

285
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:09,718
They all seemed to be sober now.
All three of them.

286
00:22:17,520 --> 00:22:20,432
- What do you think?
- I don't know.

287
00:22:20,840 --> 00:22:24,230
- Mob hit?
- I don't know.

288
00:22:24,400 --> 00:22:26,436
Could be a Mob hit.

289
00:22:26,720 --> 00:22:28,233
Yeah.

290
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:32,439
Yeah, he gets in the way
of some guy in the Mob, it's all over.

291
00:22:33,080 --> 00:22:36,516
- What do you think?
- I don't know.

292
00:22:38,520 --> 00:22:40,158
Maybe the company
he was working for.

293
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:43,278
Some kind of financial malfeasance.

294
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:46,800
- Could be a cover-up.
- Could be.

295
00:22:47,520 --> 00:22:50,034
Yeah. I think it could be a cover-up.

296
00:22:50,200 --> 00:22:54,398
Well, we need to back up
what we think

297
00:22:54,560 --> 00:22:57,154
with something as mundane
as a few facts.

298
00:22:59,240 --> 00:23:01,879
Well, I don't know what it is,
but I'll tell you what I think.

299
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:04,239
I smell a conspiracy.

300
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:06,595
I don't know about that,

301
00:23:07,280 --> 00:23:10,955
but there is one piece of evidence
in favour of what you think.

302
00:23:11,120 --> 00:23:12,758
- What?
- Somebody's following us.

303
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:18,997
What should I do?

304
00:23:20,120 --> 00:23:21,951
Go ahead, easy.

305
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:27,116
Okay, hit the brakes.

306
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:41,630
What did I do that for?

307
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:44,919
So I could get
his licence plate number.

308
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:12,959
See what you can find out
about that car that was following us.

309
00:24:13,120 --> 00:24:14,872
There's a phone booth.

310
00:24:15,040 --> 00:24:18,510
Use your influence.
I'll be in this divorce factory.

311
00:24:26,840 --> 00:24:28,956
We must get 40-, 50 divorces
through here every day.

312
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:30,633
- It's a big-volume business.
- Well,

313
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:34,475
surely you could look it up
as a professional courtesy.

314
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:37,950
I've handled divorces.
Not many anymore.

315
00:24:38,600 --> 00:24:40,397
Maybe I could refer.

316
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:44,314
Well, Mr. Matlock, for you.

317
00:24:45,960 --> 00:24:49,236
Let's see here. Palmer.

318
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:52,474
Paid cash.

319
00:24:53,360 --> 00:24:54,679
Paid cash?

320
00:24:54,840 --> 00:24:57,400
Cash for the complaint
and filing fees.

321
00:24:57,560 --> 00:25:00,836
Most of our clients
are on the easy-payment programme.

322
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:04,709
Oh, another thing.
He wanted it filed the very next day.

323
00:25:04,880 --> 00:25:07,075
I remember he made a big deal
about that.

324
00:25:07,240 --> 00:25:09,390
Well, that must have
put you under the gun,

325
00:25:09,560 --> 00:25:12,597
- grinding all that paperwork out.
- Simplicity itself.

326
00:25:12,760 --> 00:25:15,433
Palmer didn't want anything
except out.

327
00:25:17,640 --> 00:25:22,077
Well, just to make sure,
take a look at this.

328
00:25:27,800 --> 00:25:29,119
You don't recognise him?

329
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:31,636
No, that isn't the fellow
who come in here.

330
00:25:31,800 --> 00:25:33,153
What'd he look like?

331
00:25:35,120 --> 00:25:37,270
He was a little taller than me,

332
00:25:37,440 --> 00:25:42,833
which would make him about 5'1 0'',
1 60, blond.

333
00:25:44,440 --> 00:25:45,953
I can't thank you enough.

334
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:47,872
- You've been a great help.
- You will send

335
00:25:48,040 --> 00:25:50,793
- some of those referrals, yes, sir?
- Oh, yes.

336
00:25:53,920 --> 00:25:57,708
Well, our divorcee's
not Glen Palmer.

337
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:00,155
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

338
00:26:00,320 --> 00:26:01,673
What'd you find out about the car?

339
00:26:01,840 --> 00:26:05,276
It belongs to a Delaware corporation.
I'm trying to find out more about it.

340
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:07,795
Some kind of holding company.

341
00:26:08,040 --> 00:26:10,429
Some kind of holding company.

342
00:26:26,360 --> 00:26:28,590
So, what do you think?

343
00:26:28,760 --> 00:26:31,274
I don't know. What do you think?

344
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:35,273
I think it was some kind of scheme.

345
00:26:35,760 --> 00:26:37,193
Well,

346
00:26:37,960 --> 00:26:41,077
I keep thinking about
what that flight attendant told us.

347
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:43,800
- About the three guys?
- Yeah.

348
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:47,198
Two guys go into the house

349
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:50,397
like they're supporting another guy,
a third guy.

350
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:53,196
And then a short time later,

351
00:26:53,360 --> 00:26:55,510
three guys come
walking out of the house.

352
00:26:56,960 --> 00:27:00,999
What if the guy
who was being supported

353
00:27:01,160 --> 00:27:04,596
into the house was not the same guy
that came walking out of the house?

354
00:27:04,760 --> 00:27:08,355
What if Glen Palmer
was not the guy

355
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:10,158
who was being supported
into the house?

356
00:27:10,320 --> 00:27:13,278
What if Glen Palmer
was one of the three guys

357
00:27:13,440 --> 00:27:16,193
that came walking out of the house?

358
00:27:17,600 --> 00:27:18,999
A switch.

359
00:27:21,880 --> 00:27:24,314
- That's some theory.
- Yeah.

360
00:27:25,080 --> 00:27:26,638
Road.

361
00:27:41,640 --> 00:27:44,029
- Afternoon, Ben.
- Hello, Lewis.

362
00:27:44,200 --> 00:27:45,349
You here on business?

363
00:27:45,520 --> 00:27:48,159
I don't come to the morgue for fun,
Lew.

364
00:27:48,320 --> 00:27:51,517
I'm just here getting a little
expert testimony for the Palmer case.

365
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:53,910
- How's it going?
- Fair.

366
00:27:54,520 --> 00:27:55,748
What are you doing here?

367
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:57,831
I thought the medical examiner
was supposed to be

368
00:27:58,000 --> 00:27:59,433
on the golf course in the afternoon.

369
00:27:59,600 --> 00:28:01,795
Well, Ben, sometimes work
interferes with my pleasures.

370
00:28:01,960 --> 00:28:03,996
Oh, Lewis, a man should never
allow that to happen.

371
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:06,958
- Oh, I fight it, Ben. I fight it.
- Yeah.

372
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:09,714
- Take care, now.
- You too.

373
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:11,552
Thank you.

374
00:28:12,560 --> 00:28:14,391
- What'd you find out?
- Not even a close call.

375
00:28:14,560 --> 00:28:16,152
These aren't remotely
the same teeth.

376
00:28:16,320 --> 00:28:18,151
- You sure?
- Absolutely.

377
00:28:18,320 --> 00:28:21,312
For one thing, I put a bridge in
for Mr. Palmer less than a month ago,

378
00:28:21,480 --> 00:28:24,199
and the deceased in there
has perfect teeth.

379
00:28:24,360 --> 00:28:26,396
Thank you for coming down, doctor.

380
00:28:26,560 --> 00:28:28,232
I'm gonna need you
to testify in court.

381
00:28:28,400 --> 00:28:30,231
Oh, I understand.

382
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:49,111
Okay, Harry, okay,
but I need this right away. Stay on it.

383
00:28:49,280 --> 00:28:50,633
Well?

384
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:54,509
- It's not Glen Palmer in the morgue.
- You're kidding. Who is it?

385
00:28:54,680 --> 00:28:57,194
- Where's Palmer?
- Beats me.

386
00:28:57,360 --> 00:28:59,396
Wait a minute.

387
00:28:59,920 --> 00:29:02,957
I get it. Insurance fraud.

388
00:29:03,120 --> 00:29:05,111
She does three years,
he comes out of the woodwork,

389
00:29:05,280 --> 00:29:08,989
they both go flying to Rio
with half a mil from the policy.

390
00:29:09,320 --> 00:29:14,394
Danny, if she goes to prison,
she won't get the money.

391
00:29:15,120 --> 00:29:17,315
Well, what's it all about then?

392
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:21,268
I don't know, but there are gonna be
some very surprised people

393
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:22,998
in the courtroom tomorrow.

394
00:29:23,160 --> 00:29:25,151
Wait a minute,
we don't know who's behind all this.

395
00:29:25,320 --> 00:29:26,799
That's right.

396
00:29:26,960 --> 00:29:28,757
But what about the three guys?

397
00:29:28,920 --> 00:29:31,229
Who were they? Especially the one
who burned in the fire?

398
00:29:31,400 --> 00:29:34,631
- Who was that?
- I don't know.

399
00:29:34,800 --> 00:29:36,358
That's for
the Atlanta Police Department

400
00:29:36,520 --> 00:29:38,556
and the examiner to find out.

401
00:29:41,560 --> 00:29:44,597
Well, you've been a big help, son.

402
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:55,079
I don't understand.
You're telling me that Glen is alive?

403
00:29:55,800 --> 00:29:57,313
It certainly seems that way.

404
00:29:58,160 --> 00:29:59,878
Where is he?

405
00:30:01,200 --> 00:30:03,998
Is there anything about all this
you haven't told me?

406
00:30:05,160 --> 00:30:08,232
No. What is going on?

407
00:30:10,560 --> 00:30:13,199
We will find the answers.

408
00:30:20,960 --> 00:30:23,394
Yeah. Hi, Harry.

409
00:30:25,320 --> 00:30:26,594
You got it.

410
00:30:27,880 --> 00:30:30,348
Treadway Corporation.

411
00:30:30,520 --> 00:30:32,590
Listen, thanks. I owe you.

412
00:30:32,760 --> 00:30:34,796
Right, bye.

413
00:30:36,480 --> 00:30:37,674
Your editor wants to know when

414
00:30:37,840 --> 00:30:40,195
- the Palmer story's gonna be ready.
- It's almost ready.

415
00:30:40,360 --> 00:30:43,636
- Here it is. Treadway Corporation.
- Well, what am I supposed to tell him?

416
00:30:43,800 --> 00:30:46,155
Tell him he'll get it when it's ready.

417
00:30:46,320 --> 00:30:49,118
Are you telling me
that my brother's alive?

418
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:53,432
That this is all some elaborate scheme
to defraud an insurance company?

419
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:55,511
Glen would never
do something like that.

420
00:30:55,680 --> 00:30:59,195
- May I quote you on that?
- Damn right you can.

421
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:04,993
Look, I lost my brother in that fire,

422
00:31:05,160 --> 00:31:06,991
and I think that wife of his killed him.

423
00:31:08,960 --> 00:31:13,317
Mr. Palmer, where would Glen go
if he wanted to hide?

424
00:31:13,480 --> 00:31:14,879
How the hell do I know?

425
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:16,871
Did he have a favourite
South American country?

426
00:31:19,040 --> 00:31:21,190
I think you better leave.

427
00:31:26,720 --> 00:31:29,598
You're a local sales rep
with the Treadway Corporation.

428
00:31:29,760 --> 00:31:32,274
Would you mind explaining
why a Treadway car was following

429
00:31:32,440 --> 00:31:34,510
Mr. Matlock and me?

430
00:31:38,760 --> 00:31:39,749
Get out of here.

431
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:41,638
I bet if I call
the Treadway Corporation,

432
00:31:41,800 --> 00:31:43,950
I'd find out it was your car
that was following us.

433
00:31:44,120 --> 00:31:45,109
I said get out of here.

434
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:49,159
You're in this with your brother,
aren't--? Thanks for your cooperation.

435
00:31:52,560 --> 00:31:54,596
What do you wanna do about him?

436
00:31:59,640 --> 00:32:03,030
Now, Dr. Randolph, you were
Glen Palmer's dentist, weren't you?

437
00:32:03,200 --> 00:32:05,430
- Yes. Yes, I was.
- And in that capacity,

438
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:09,354
I asked you to bring your x-rays
of his teeth to court with you today.

439
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:12,557
- Did you do that, sir?
- I've got them right here.

440
00:32:13,640 --> 00:32:15,392
These have been shown to counsel,
Your Honour,

441
00:32:15,560 --> 00:32:18,028
and I ask that they be marked
Defence Exhibit D

442
00:32:18,200 --> 00:32:20,839
- and entered into evidence.
- So ordered.

443
00:32:21,400 --> 00:32:23,231
Dr. Randolph, did you have occasion

444
00:32:23,400 --> 00:32:26,631
to visit the county morgue
and examine the remains of the body

445
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:28,199
found in the Palmer home?

446
00:32:28,360 --> 00:32:29,588
I did.

447
00:32:29,760 --> 00:32:31,910
Would you tell the court
what you learned

448
00:32:32,080 --> 00:32:35,072
when you compared
your dental records of Glen Palmer

449
00:32:35,240 --> 00:32:37,071
with the remains?

450
00:32:37,640 --> 00:32:42,191
I examined the teeth of the deceased
against my x-rays for Glen Palmer.

451
00:32:42,840 --> 00:32:43,955
They were a perfect match.

452
00:32:45,880 --> 00:32:46,869
Say that again.

453
00:32:47,040 --> 00:32:51,192
I positively identified them
as being those of Mr. Palmer.

454
00:32:53,040 --> 00:32:57,431
Dr. Randolph,
when we were both at the morgue,

455
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:01,832
you told me the remains
were not Glen Palmer's, didn't you?

456
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:03,069
Objection.

457
00:33:03,240 --> 00:33:05,515
Mr. Matlock is cross-examining
his own witness.

458
00:33:05,680 --> 00:33:08,513
I'm hearing a different testimony
than I heard at the morgue.

459
00:33:08,680 --> 00:33:12,070
Overruled.
Mr. Matlock, you may proceed.

460
00:33:12,240 --> 00:33:15,152
Didn't you tell me a different story
at the morgue?

461
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:19,871
Yes, but you pressed me
to say that it wasn't Mr. Palmer,

462
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:21,792
but it clearly was.

463
00:33:21,960 --> 00:33:24,918
You didn't tell me
that Mr. Palmer had a bridge,

464
00:33:25,080 --> 00:33:28,629
- but the deceased did not?
- He didn't have a bridge.

465
00:33:28,800 --> 00:33:31,951
Just look at my x-rays
against the coroner's.

466
00:33:32,120 --> 00:33:35,795
- They're a perfect match.
- I see.

467
00:33:35,960 --> 00:33:38,394
Then I have one last question
for you.

468
00:33:38,560 --> 00:33:39,629
Who got to you?

469
00:33:39,800 --> 00:33:42,633
Who paid you to perjure yourself
in court today?

470
00:33:42,800 --> 00:33:45,872
Objection.
Mr. Matlock is grossly argumentative.

471
00:33:46,040 --> 00:33:49,589
Withdraw the question.
I have no further use of this jackass.

472
00:33:49,760 --> 00:33:51,512
Witness.

473
00:35:37,320 --> 00:35:38,753
Mr. Palmer?

474
00:35:41,680 --> 00:35:43,033
Glen Palmer?

475
00:35:43,200 --> 00:35:46,590
I've got a message from your wife.
She's in a lot of--

476
00:35:59,920 --> 00:36:01,751
Palmer, your wife's on trial
for murdering you.

477
00:36:01,920 --> 00:36:03,353
- Shut up.
- Your brother set her up.

478
00:36:03,520 --> 00:36:05,033
I said shut up, and I mean it.

479
00:36:05,200 --> 00:36:07,270
Wait a minute.
Is he telling the truth?

480
00:36:07,440 --> 00:36:08,759
Glen, get back to your room.

481
00:36:08,920 --> 00:36:10,751
I'm a reporter.
My press pass is in my wallet.

482
00:36:10,920 --> 00:36:14,037
Get him to show you a newspaper.
Laurie's on the front page.

483
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:16,555
You promised me
Laurie was gonna to be okay.

484
00:36:16,720 --> 00:36:17,835
- She is.
- Like hell she is.

485
00:36:18,000 --> 00:36:20,355
She's on trial for murdering you.

486
00:36:21,480 --> 00:36:23,436
Who are these guys?

487
00:36:23,880 --> 00:36:25,108
They're federal agents.

488
00:36:26,800 --> 00:36:29,678
- Well, what are they holding you for?
- They're not.

489
00:36:29,840 --> 00:36:32,673
Well, then let's go.
Laurie's sitting in jail right now.

490
00:36:32,840 --> 00:36:36,515
Glen, there are people out there
looking to kill you.

491
00:36:37,080 --> 00:36:38,832
I'll take my chances.

492
00:36:54,440 --> 00:36:55,759
You can't stop me.

493
00:36:57,640 --> 00:36:59,358
Understand?

494
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:02,231
You signed the death certificate,
Lewis.

495
00:37:02,400 --> 00:37:04,709
You certified the identity
of those remains in there

496
00:37:04,880 --> 00:37:06,950
- as being Glen Palmer.
- That's my job.

497
00:37:07,120 --> 00:37:09,793
I know for a fact
those remains are not Glen Palmer.

498
00:37:09,960 --> 00:37:11,313
Why'd you say they are?

499
00:37:11,480 --> 00:37:14,278
- I'm sorry, Ben, I can't discuss it.
- The hell you can't.

500
00:37:14,440 --> 00:37:17,557
I just had an expert witness
perjure himself in court.

501
00:37:17,720 --> 00:37:20,837
First, I thought he was bought off,
but then I got to thinking about you.

502
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:24,029
You had to know
it wasn't Glen Palmer.

503
00:37:24,200 --> 00:37:26,350
Now, I know you'd rather play golf
than work, Lewis,

504
00:37:26,520 --> 00:37:27,919
but I know you can't be bought.

505
00:37:28,080 --> 00:37:32,756
- It had to have taken something else.
- Ben, I've got nothing to say.

506
00:37:34,120 --> 00:37:36,076
Michael Palmer
made the identification.

507
00:37:36,240 --> 00:37:38,674
He was the one who convinced you
to lie, wasn't he?

508
00:37:38,840 --> 00:37:40,319
Ben, I can't help you.

509
00:37:40,480 --> 00:37:43,040
You've got to, Lewis. I don't know
what these people are up to,

510
00:37:43,200 --> 00:37:44,918
but I can tell you what they've done.

511
00:37:45,080 --> 00:37:46,354
They've framed an innocent woman.

512
00:37:46,520 --> 00:37:48,238
I know that
as sure as I'm standing here,

513
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:49,992
but if I can't prove it in court,

514
00:37:50,160 --> 00:37:52,674
that girl's gonna go to prison
for a crime she didn't commit,

515
00:37:52,840 --> 00:37:56,515
and I'm not gonna let that happen,
Lewis, and neither are you.

516
00:38:13,120 --> 00:38:16,078
- Mr. Palmer.
- Yes, Mr. Matlock?

517
00:38:16,240 --> 00:38:17,719
Yeah.

518
00:38:20,800 --> 00:38:23,633
I think I have a fair idea
of what's going on.

519
00:38:23,800 --> 00:38:25,950
- Really?
- Yeah.

520
00:38:26,320 --> 00:38:30,313
I want you to call this thing off,
Mr. Palmer, or whatever your name is.

521
00:38:30,480 --> 00:38:32,710
I won't allow this woman
to go to prison.

522
00:38:42,040 --> 00:38:44,235
Mr. Palmer,

523
00:38:44,400 --> 00:38:49,349
I listened with real interest just now

524
00:38:49,960 --> 00:38:53,555
while you testified
about your brother's death.

525
00:38:54,040 --> 00:38:57,191
It must have been
a terrible blow to you.

526
00:38:58,280 --> 00:38:59,872
Glen and I were very close.

527
00:39:01,720 --> 00:39:06,953
The only problem with your testimony,
it was all lies.

528
00:39:07,120 --> 00:39:08,269
Objection.

529
00:39:08,440 --> 00:39:10,192
Counsel is badgering the witness,
Your Honour.

530
00:39:10,360 --> 00:39:13,477
On the contrary, Your Honour,
I haven't even begun yet.

531
00:39:13,640 --> 00:39:14,834
Sustained.

532
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:16,718
Your name is not Michael Palmer,
is it?

533
00:39:19,320 --> 00:39:21,117
I don't know
what you're talking about.

534
00:39:21,280 --> 00:39:22,793
Neither does the prosecution,
Your Honour.

535
00:39:22,960 --> 00:39:24,678
I assure the court
this is most relevant,

536
00:39:24,840 --> 00:39:26,717
and I'll connect it up momentarily.

537
00:39:26,880 --> 00:39:28,518
See that you do, Mr. Matlock.

538
00:39:28,680 --> 00:39:30,796
You work for the Justice Department,
don't you?

539
00:39:31,280 --> 00:39:33,714
No, I sell paper goods
on commission.

540
00:39:33,880 --> 00:39:35,757
You work for
the Witness Protection Programme

541
00:39:35,920 --> 00:39:38,150
of the Justice Department,
don't you?

542
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:39,435
Not true.

543
00:39:39,600 --> 00:39:41,272
And you set my client's husband up

544
00:39:41,440 --> 00:39:43,317
with a new identity here in Atlanta,
didn't you?

545
00:39:43,480 --> 00:39:44,833
None of this is true.

546
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:47,468
Then for some reason,
you needed to pull him out of this life

547
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:50,757
and place him somewhere else,
so you staged his death.

548
00:39:50,920 --> 00:39:54,595
No, and your allegations
are irresponsible.

549
00:39:54,760 --> 00:39:57,035
I'm not out to compromise
your operation.

550
00:39:57,200 --> 00:40:00,988
All I want from you
is a little cooperation and candour

551
00:40:01,160 --> 00:40:05,073
so that I can get this woman
out from under a charge of murder

552
00:40:05,240 --> 00:40:09,199
which she did not commit,
and which, in fact, never occurred.

553
00:40:09,360 --> 00:40:12,193
Mr. Matlock,
can you substantiate any of this?

554
00:40:12,360 --> 00:40:16,069
Yes, Your Honour.
That's what I intend to do right now.

555
00:40:16,240 --> 00:40:18,800
Would Dr. Weeks please stand?

556
00:40:20,280 --> 00:40:24,034
This is Dr. Lewis Weeks.
Do you recognise him?

557
00:40:25,960 --> 00:40:29,191
- No, I do not.
- Is that so, sir?

558
00:40:29,360 --> 00:40:31,954
Didn't you go to Dr. Weeks

559
00:40:32,120 --> 00:40:34,475
and identify yourself
as a government agent?

560
00:40:35,080 --> 00:40:36,354
No, it is not true.

561
00:40:36,520 --> 00:40:40,672
Then you told Dr. Weeks
that you were on a secret assignment,

562
00:40:40,840 --> 00:40:44,719
and you ordered him not to make
his usual identification tests

563
00:40:44,880 --> 00:40:46,233
on a badly burned corpse,

564
00:40:46,400 --> 00:40:50,518
and to identify that corpse
as one Glen Palmer.

565
00:40:50,680 --> 00:40:51,954
That's a lie.

566
00:40:52,120 --> 00:40:55,954
I warn you, sir, a government agent
is not above the law,

567
00:40:56,120 --> 00:40:58,076
and I will see you in prison

568
00:40:58,240 --> 00:41:00,196
if you continue to perjure yourself.

569
00:41:01,120 --> 00:41:03,793
Your Honour,
may I see you in chambers?

570
00:41:03,960 --> 00:41:06,952
Your Honour, I demand the right
to set the record straight

571
00:41:07,120 --> 00:41:10,510
and clear this innocent woman
before that man leaves the stand.

572
00:41:10,680 --> 00:41:13,558
Well, I see no reason
to stop this examination. Proceed.

573
00:41:13,720 --> 00:41:15,233
Thank you.

574
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:19,549
Mr. Palmer,

575
00:41:19,720 --> 00:41:23,633
an employee of the federal government
has a sacred trust.

576
00:41:24,320 --> 00:41:28,598
You, above all people,
must never break the law.

577
00:41:29,640 --> 00:41:30,993
You know what I think?

578
00:41:31,160 --> 00:41:33,913
I think the Attorney General
of the United States

579
00:41:34,080 --> 00:41:36,469
would never condone
what you've done here.

580
00:41:36,640 --> 00:41:39,108
I don't think your superiors
even know about it.

581
00:41:39,280 --> 00:41:42,750
You took it on yourself
to break the law.

582
00:41:43,240 --> 00:41:46,277
You placed a corpse
in the Palmer home,

583
00:41:46,440 --> 00:41:47,793
and then you burned it.

584
00:41:48,320 --> 00:41:50,515
Do you have any idea
what you're doing?

585
00:41:50,680 --> 00:41:52,796
Then you framed Laurie Palmer.

586
00:41:52,960 --> 00:41:57,750
You took out an insurance policy.
You filed their divorce.

587
00:41:57,920 --> 00:42:03,233
In the name of justice,
you perpetrated the greatest injustice.

588
00:42:03,400 --> 00:42:06,790
You took away the liberty
of an innocent person.

589
00:42:07,320 --> 00:42:10,471
We are in a kind of war in this country,
Mr. Matlock,

590
00:42:10,640 --> 00:42:13,598
and you may not like it,
and you may not approve,

591
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:17,116
but we have to do
whatever is necessary in order to win.

592
00:42:17,640 --> 00:42:19,119
I've been at this for 1 5 years,

593
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:21,953
and I've got the scars to prove
that I put my life on the line

594
00:42:22,120 --> 00:42:23,109
for what I believe in.

595
00:42:23,280 --> 00:42:25,874
And what about you,
Mr. Matlock, huh?

596
00:42:28,280 --> 00:42:31,716
Do you care about the years of work
that you're ruining,

597
00:42:31,880 --> 00:42:34,872
the lives that you're putting
in jeopardy?

598
00:42:44,160 --> 00:42:46,037
Yes, I do.

599
00:42:51,720 --> 00:42:54,109
But people like you...

600
00:42:55,240 --> 00:42:57,800
...will destroy this country...

601
00:42:59,080 --> 00:43:04,757
...and everything it represents
in your effort to save it.

602
00:43:08,160 --> 00:43:10,674
Now, let's end this charade.

603
00:43:11,440 --> 00:43:13,874
Glen Palmer's alive, isn't he?

604
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:17,149
Yes.

605
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:19,436
And you do work for
the federal government, don't you?

606
00:43:20,160 --> 00:43:21,149
Yes.

607
00:43:21,320 --> 00:43:23,959
And you did stage
Glen Palmer's death, didn't you?

608
00:43:25,520 --> 00:43:28,273
- Yes.
- One last question.

609
00:43:28,840 --> 00:43:34,153
Why did you allow this woman
to be charged with murder?

610
00:43:34,320 --> 00:43:35,548
I don't think that's relevant.

611
00:43:35,720 --> 00:43:39,872
Answer the question, damn it.
Just once, tell the truth in this court.

612
00:43:40,040 --> 00:43:42,349
Because it was the most
convincing thing we could do.

613
00:43:44,560 --> 00:43:48,030
Glen was the bookkeeper
of a major drug distributor.

614
00:43:48,200 --> 00:43:51,317
- We needed him for one more trial.
- You lied to me.

615
00:43:51,800 --> 00:43:54,792
- Glen.
- Order. Order in the court.

616
00:43:54,960 --> 00:43:59,795
Your Honour, I move that all charges
against my client be dismissed.

617
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:03,639
The prosecution joins in that motion,
Your Honour.

618
00:44:03,800 --> 00:44:05,870
Motion granted. Case dismissed.

619
00:44:06,920 --> 00:44:08,797
I will see the witness
and the district attorney

620
00:44:08,960 --> 00:44:10,678
in my chambers in five minutes.

621
00:44:11,200 --> 00:44:13,760
The deputy will take you out
the back way.

622
00:44:13,920 --> 00:44:15,751
Thank you.

623
00:44:26,520 --> 00:44:27,669
Laurie, wait.

624
00:44:27,840 --> 00:44:30,991
Wait, I didn't know
what was happening to you, I swear.

625
00:44:31,160 --> 00:44:33,879
I don't know anything about you.

626
00:44:34,040 --> 00:44:36,600
I don't even know what to call you.

627
00:44:36,760 --> 00:44:38,876
Well, you will. I'll tell you everything.

628
00:44:40,000 --> 00:44:43,117
But most importantly, I love you.

629
00:44:43,680 --> 00:44:45,716
And I'm your husband.

630
00:44:47,200 --> 00:44:51,432
Yes. Yes, you are.

631
00:45:06,960 --> 00:45:08,837
Well, you got your story.

632
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:11,829
Yeah. I got a bonus too.

633
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:13,797
What are you gonna do with it,
get a new car?

634
00:45:13,960 --> 00:45:15,359
Hell, no. I don't need a new car.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
